      {
        "id": "p39-q27a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the linear relationship that relates $F$ with $C$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$F=1.8C+32$.",
          "worked": "Let $F=mC+b$. The two given temperatures give\n$$\\begin{align*}m&=\\frac{68-50}{20-10}\\\\m&=\\frac95.\\end{align*}$$\nUsing $C=10$ and $F=50$,\n$$\\begin{align*}50&=\\frac95(10)+b\\\\b&=32\\\\F&=\\frac95C+32.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      },
      {
        "id": "p39-q27b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p39-q27b-context",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Suppose a chart shows the temperature (°C) of an area throughout the course of a day. The same chart is to now display temperature in Fahrenheit instead.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p39-q27b-instruction",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "How could this chart be produced from the original one?",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Dilate the temperature axis by a factor of $1.8$, then translate $32$ units in the positive direction (up).",
          "worked": "If the Celsius chart is $C=f(t)$, then the Fahrenheit chart is\n$$\\begin{align*}F&=\\frac95f(t)+32.\\end{align*}$$\nDilate vertically by a factor of $\\frac95=1.8$, then translate up $32$ units. Keep the time coordinates unchanged and label the temperature axis in °F.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
